   "stem": {
      "text": "Let g be defined by g(x) = 2x + 3 for x < 1, g(x) = kx - 1 for 1 <= x < 4, and g(x) = x^2 - kx for x >= 4, where k is a constant. For what value of k is g continuous at x = 1?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 6
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Just to the left of x = 1 the rule is 2x + 3, so the left-hand limit at x = 1 is 5.",
         "mathjson": 5
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At x = 1 and just to its right the rule is kx - 1, so the right-hand limit and g(1) both equal k - 1.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Subtract",
            "k",
            1
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Continuity at x = 1 needs 5 = k - 1.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            5,
            [
               "Subtract",
               "k",
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So k = 6.",
         "mathjson": 6
      }
   ],
